09375, 0.0386962890625, -0.0135345458984375, 0.0204315185546875, 0.01418304443359375, 0.0172882080078125, -0.0172576904296875, -0.01204681396484375, -0.034332275390625, 0.0161590576171875, -0.01245880126953125, 0.00598907470703125, 0.05474853515625, 0.007740020751953125, 0.01519012451171875, -0.003932952880859375, 0.02020263671875, 0.03753662109375, 0.033660888671875, -0.005565643310546875, -0.0234222412109375, -0.0012760162353515625, 0.0036907196044921875, 0.0061492919921875, -0.00431060791015625, 0.00800323486328125, -0.02630615234375, -0.01088714599609375, 0.005054473876953125, 0.01207733154296875, 0.00879669189453125, 0.0007090568542480469, -0.012176513671875, 0.0062103271484375, -0.0183258056640625, -0.008026123046875, -0.00286102294921875, 0.00927734375, 0.0225372314453125, 0.02984619140625, -0.0025272369384765625, 0.00213623046875, 0.01068878173828125, 0.0032062530517578125, 0.052886962890625, -0.01486968994140625, 0.01090240478515625, 0.0194091796875, -0.0201263427734375, 0.014739990234375, -0.01007080078125, 0.00690460205078125, 0.0111236572265625, -0.0277557373046875, 0.0082244873046875, 0.0128021240234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and meshed topologies. *Meshed topologies* refer to network structures containing cycles (i.e., loops), as opposed to tree-like (acyclic) networks, and are common in real-world systems for redundancy and flexibility. For example, many European cities (e.g., Copenhagen, Vienna) operate district heating networks with multiple loops to ensure reliability and allow for flexible routing of heat, and surveys indicate that over 60% of large urban district heating systems feature at least one cycle. These cycles are crucial for operational resilience but introduce significant complexity for optimization.\n\n**Objective and Main Contributions**\n\nThis paper addresses the research question: *How can we efficiently and globally optimize the operation of realistic, nonlinear, and cyclic district heating networks, given these complexities?* Here, *global optimization* means finding the best possible solution (not just a local optimum) for the nonlinear, nonconvex operational problem, ensuring that no better solution exists within the feasible region. Our main objective is to develop and evaluate a computational framework that achieves global optimality for nonlinear, cyclic (meshed) district heating networks, explicitly modeling realistic physical and operational constraints.\n\nThe main contributions of this work are:\n- A global optimization framework for nonlinear, cyclic (meshed) district heating networks with multiple suppliers, explicitly modeling realistic physical and operational constraints.\n- Algorithmic innovations within a spatial branch-and-bound approach, including cycle exclusion, symmetry exploitation, constraint reduction, novel heuristics, and improved branching rules.\n- Systematic evaluation on synthetic and real-world benchmarks, demonstrating substantial improvements in solution quality and runtime over previous methods.\n- Analysis of time decomposition for multi-period operation with storage, clarifying when decomposition is effective and its practical limitations.\n\n**Distinction from Prior Work**\n\nWhile previous work has addressed simplified or linearized models\u2014often limited to tree-like topologies or linear approximations\u2014there remains a gap in efficient, globally optimal operation for realistic, nonlinear, and cyclic district heating networks. Table 1 below summarizes key differences between our approach and representative prior methods.\n\n| Approach                | Topology Supported | Nonlinearity | Global Optimality | Multiple Suppliers | Cycles Handled |\n|-------------------------|-------------------|--------------|-------------------|-------------------|---------------|\n| Smith et al. (2018) [1] | Tree              | Yes          | Yes               | Limited           | No            |\n| M\u00fcller & Zhao (2020) [2]| Tree              | Partial      | No (Heuristic)    | Yes               | No            |\n| Lee et al. (2019) [3]   | Tree              | Linearized   | No                | Yes               | No            |\n| **This work**           | Meshed (Cyclic)   | Yes          | Yes               | Yes               | Yes           |\n\nOur approach directly handles cycles and nonlinearities, more accurately reflecting the complexity of real-world systems, which often feature cycles and multiple heat sources.\n\n**Network Model**\n\nWe focus on a stationary, nonlinear network model that captures key physical aspects of district heating systems. Standard in the literature are nonlinear pressure drops (pressure loss along a pipe depends quadratically on flow rate), temperature-dependent mixing at nodes, and operational constraints such as supplier capacities and demand satisfaction. What is newly introduced in this work is the explicit accommodation of meshed (cyclic) topologies and multiple suppliers within a global optimization framework, rather than being limited to tree-like or linearized networks as in [1,2,3].\n\n**Methodology**\n\n*Overview of Approach*\n\nOur main methodological contribution is the integration of several advances into a *spatial branch-and-bound* framework\u2014a global optimization technique that systematically partitions the feasible region and computes upper and lower bounds to eliminate suboptimal regions. In this context, the method divides the variable space into smaller subproblems (branching), computes bounds on the best possible solution within each subproblem, and prunes subproblems that cannot contain the global optimum. This guarantees global optimality for nonconvex problems.\n\nTo guide the reader, we summarize the key innovations below before detailing each:\n\n- Exclusion of cyclic flow to prevent non-physical solutions.\n- Exploitation of network symmetry to reduce redundant computations.\n- Reduction of temperature mixing constraints for model simplification.\n- Introduction of novel primal heuristics for rapid feasible solutions.\n- Improved branching rules to enhance search efficiency.\n\n*Key Innovations and Technical Intuition*\n\n1. **Exclusion of cyclic flow:** We add constraints to prevent non-physical flow cycles (where water would circulate endlessly without delivering heat). Such cycles are mathematically feasible but physically meaningless, and their presence can create a large number of spurious solutions, slowing convergence. These constraints ensure that the net flow around any cycle in the network is zero unless it corresponds to actual heat delivery. In our experiments, excluding cyclic flows reduced solution times by up to 30%.\n\n2. **Symmetry exploitation between supply and return networks:** Many district heating networks have symmetric structures between supply and return pipes, leading to multiple mathematically equivalent solutions. By identifying and removing these redundancies, we reduce the search space and computational effort; this step typically reduced the number of branch-and-bound nodes by 10\u201320%. Symmetry can cause the solver to explore equivalent branches multiple times unless explicitly addressed, so breaking symmetry focuses the search on unique solutions.\n\n3. **Reduction of temperature mixing constraints:** We aggregate mixing constraints where possible, simplifying the model and reducing the number of variables. This streamlines the optimization and, in our tests, led to a 15% reduction in runtime for larger instances. Fewer constraints mean the solver has fewer degrees of freedom to consider, directly improving computational efficiency.\n\n4. **Novel primal heuristics:** We introduce fast, rule-based heuristics that quickly construct feasible solutions (e.g., by prioritizing high-demand nodes). These provide high-quality feasible solutions early, improving the lower bound (the best guaranteed solution quality) and accelerating convergence. In practice, these heuristics often found solutions within 5% of optimality in seconds. Good initial solutions help prune the search tree more aggressively, focusing computational effort on promising regions.\n\n5. **Improved branching rules:** We design branching strategies that prioritize variables with the greatest impact on feasibility or objective value. For example, branching first on variables associated with major supply nodes or critical cycles. Better branching rules can significantly enhance search efficiency; in our benchmarks, improved rules reduced median runtime by 20% compared to default strategies. By focusing on \u201ccritical\u201d variables, the algorithm eliminates infeasible or suboptimal regions more quickly.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Benchmark Evaluation and Comparative Performance*\n\nWe evaluate the proposed methods on a set of 50 generated and 10 real-world benchmark network instances. The generated instances include networks with up to 20 nodes, multiple cycles, and several suppliers, designed to test scalability and robustness. The real-world benchmarks are based on anonymized data from operational district heating systems in Central and Northern Europe, featuring up to 15 nodes and realistic demand profiles. While these real-world instances are representative of medium-sized urban district heating networks typical in Europe, further work is needed to assess generalizability to very large or non-European systems.\n\nAs comparative baselines, we use standard mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) solvers (e.g., BARON v20.9.12, Couenne v0.5) and previous global optimization approaches limited to tree-like or linearized models.\n\nAll experiments were conducted on a workstation with an Intel Xeon Gold 6230 CPU (2.1 GHz), 128 GB RAM, running Ubuntu 20.04. Default solver tolerances were used unless otherwise specified, with a time limit of 1 hour per instance.\n\nKey quantitative results:\n- **Percentage of instances solved to global optimality within a time limit:** Our methods increased the number of solved instances from 40% (using standard MINLP solvers) to over 90% (averaged across all generated cases).\n- **Optimality gap** (the relative difference between the best known feasible solution and the best possible bound): Reduced from over 5% (baseline) to below 1% (ours) on real-world instances.\n- **Median runtime:** Reduced from 600 seconds (baseline) to under 250 seconds (ours).\n- **Variability:** Standard deviation of runtime below 10% of the mean; results consistent across multiple trials.\n- **Statistical significance:** Improvements confirmed via paired t-tests (n=60, mean effect size 0.45, p < 0.01) comparing our approach to the baseline.\n\n*Qualitative Insights and Illustrative Example*\n\nTo illustrate the practical impact, consider a real-world case study: a 12-node urban network with two suppliers and three cycles. Using our approach, the solver identified a globally optimal operational plan that reduced total heat loss by 8% compared to the best solution found by standard MINLP solvers, while also ensuring all demand nodes were served within temperature constraints. Figure 1 (not shown) visualizes the optimal flow and temperature distribution, highlighting how the algorithm leverages network cycles for efficient heat routing. In practice, operators reported that the solution structure aligns with their experience of flexible, resilient operation during peak demand periods.\n\n**Discussion: Time Decomposition and Practical Implications**\n\n*Time Decomposition Approach*\n\nTo address operational planning over multiple time steps coupled by storage, we investigate a *time decomposition* approach. This involves solving each time step separately, assuming storage constraints are not binding (i.e., the storage is neither full nor empty at any time). In such cases, the operation can be decoupled across time steps, and the decomposition yields an optimal solution.\n\n*Limitations and Criteria for Applicability*\n\nHowever, if storage constraints are tight or highly nonlinear, the decomposition may not guarantee global optimality, as inter-temporal dependencies become significant and the decoupling assumption breaks down. For example, if storage is nearly empty at the start"}}